@country: USA
@bio:

Craig Sapp is a leading researcher in music encoding and
computational music analysis. He earned a Ph.D. in
computer-based music theory and acoustics from Stanford
University and has taught at Peabody Conservatory and
Stanford, while also working at Royal Holloway on
computational studies of Chopin’s mazurkas with Nicholas
Cook. He is an adjunct professor at Stanford and a
researcher at the Center for Computer-Assisted Research in
the Humanities of the Packard Humanities Institute. Sapp
directs technical work for the Josquin Research Project,
Tasso in Music, and several international digital music
initiatives involving Renaissance, Polish, chant, and
twentieth-century repertories.

@@END: BIO